  "model19": "Flexible growth and operating timelines",
  "model20": "User-selected analyst path, not issuer guidance or a fitted forecast",
  "model21": "One initial-growth draw shifts every explicit annual growth rate by the same percentage-point difference from the baseline; the fade then reaches fixed stable growth. No annual independent draws or clipping.",
  "model22": "Constant WACC throughout the forecast and terminal period; no risk or capital-structure fade is modeled.",
  "model23": "Revenue growth, EBIT margin and capital efficiency have independent timelines. Completed transitions hold their mature settings; the explicit forecast continues until all three have matured. Capital intensity fades from initial sales/capital to (1 + stable growth) × mature margin × (1 − tax) / terminal ROIC. Reinvestment still funds actual incremental revenue, so growth edits can change capital spending without changing capital efficiency. Terminal ROIC remains a separate judgment.",
  "model24": "Flexible growth and maturity",
  "model25": "Growth reaches stable growth in the selected maturity year. Margin converges from normalized starting EBIT to its target over the full horizon. Initial incremental capital intensity is held through the explicit phase, then fades to (1 + stable growth) × mature margin × (1 − tax) / terminal ROIC. Synchronizing margin and capital maturity is a modeling simplification; terminal ROIC remains a separate judgment.",
  "model26": "Staged capacity research case",
  "model27": "Explicit analyst path, not issuer guidance or a fitted forecast",
  "model28": "Front-loaded growth, profitability reached during the buildout, and capital productivity initially doubling, then capital intensity fading to (1 + terminal growth) × mature margin × (1 − tax) / terminal ROIC, matching the protected engine’s terminal timing. Current-year guidance and capacity plans support the staged structure; each annual point remains an analyst assumption.",
  "model29": "The flexible path requires a finite starting EBIT normalization.",
  "model30": "Flexible maturity must be 10, 15 or 20 years.",
  "model31": "The flexible path requires a positive mature margin for finite terminal-consistent sales/capital.",
